# Local dev through Portless

`pnpm dev:portless` starts every app behind Portless with fixed URLs:

| App | URL |
| --- | --- |
| apps/marketing | `https://www.localhost` (proxies `/docs` to the docs app) |
| apps/docs | `https://docs.localhost/docs` |
| apps/examples/nextjs | `https://example.localhost` |

In a linked git worktree, Portless puts the last segment of the branch name
in front of each host: on `perf/dx-build-ci` the marketing site is
`https://dx-build-ci.www.localhost`. `pnpm portless get www` prints the URL for
the current checkout, and two worktrees never take over each other's hosts.

Supabase runs on ports the CLI picks for each checkout and branch; `pnpm supabase:start` writes `SUPABASE_URL` and the checkout's site URLs to `.env.development.local`.

A new worktree runs `node scripts/worktree-setup.ts` (Cursor runs it from
`.cursor/worktrees.json`): it installs from the pnpm store and copies the main
checkout's env files without its stack keys. Add `--db` to start the
worktree's own stack. The docs app keeps port 3001, so only one checkout at a
time can run it.

- Open and test pages only through these URLs, with `agent-browser`. Never
  guess a `localhost:<port>`, and never use the IDE's built-in browser.
- Reuse the `dev:portless` that is already running. Never kill or restart it
  without asking; `pnpm dev:cleanup` removes stale routes.
- The nextjs example signs in with the seeded users `admin@acme.test` and
  `member@acme.test`, both with password `password123`.
- Unit and integration runs do not need Portless.
